# CQRS Application Slices

New Application use cases follow the existing vertical-slice layout. One folder = one command or query.

## Folder layout

```
{Feature}/
  Commands/{Name}/
    {Name}Command.cs
    {Name}CommandHandler.cs
    {Name}CommandValidator.cs   # FluentValidation when input needs rules
    {Name}Result.cs             # optional result type
  Queries/{Name}/
    {Name}Query.cs
    {Name}QueryHandler.cs
    {Name}QueryValidator.cs     # optional
  Dtos/
  Mappings/
```

Examples: `Reservations/Commands/CreateReservation/`, `Reports/Queries/GetManagerStats/`.

## Rules

- Command/Query is a `sealed record` implementing `IRequest<T>` (MediatR).
- Handler is a `sealed class` with constructor-injected Application interfaces only (`I*Repository`, `IUnitOfWork`, etc.).
- **No EF Core, DbContext, or Infrastructure types** in Application.
- Put shared contracts in `Common/Interfaces/`; feature DTOs in `{Feature}/Dtos/`; mapping extensions in `{Feature}/Mappings/`.
- Controllers stay thin: validate auth/HTTP, then `_mediator.Send(...)`.
- Pure calculators (e.g. `ManagerStatsCalculator`) may live next to the feature, outside the slice folder, when reused/tested independently.

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD
public sealed record GetManagerStatsQuery(DateTime? FromUtc, DateTime? ToUtc)
    : IRequest<ManagerStatsDto>;

// ❌ BAD — EF / DbContext in handler, or dumping logic into the controller
```
